many interesting things happen during  the 1980s one of those things was the  launching in 1983 of a large randomized  trial on folic acid and birth defects  this was the MRC vitamin study which  included women from 33 countries who  were planning to get pregnant amazingly  the risk of neural tube defects a type  of birth defect was reduced by 70% in  the group assigned to folic acid and  this was a big deal because newer tube  defects are serious birth defects that  happen when the central nervous system  does not close in the first 28 days of  pregnancy and this lack of closure can  lead to very serious disabilities or  death so the findings from the trial  were used to support major changes in  public health policy in the United  States the Centers for Disease Control  advises the all women should take point  for milligrams of folic acid every day  before getting pregnant and many  countries have implemented mandatory  fortification of wheat flour with folic  acid so women can get their daily dose  of folic acid from their food however  the emergency vitamin trial left some  questions unanswered first the study  only included women with history of  pregnancies affected by new activity  effects so would folic acid work for the  rest of the woman and second the study  gave women high doses of Pollock acid  with the same effect we seen with the  lower and possibly safer doses that were  usually included in more in multivitamin  supplements since no randomized trials  were available to answer these questions  investigators did what they always do  they analyzed data from observational  studies and one of those studies was the  Sloane epidemiology unit birth defects  study at Boston University this  observational study included pregnancies  from several North American cities in  the 1990s and this study estimated that  the risk of birth defect was 35 percent  lower among pregnancies exposed to folic  acid 35% lower that's a strong  association can we then conclude the  folic acid at the usual doses lowers the  risk of neural tube defects by 35  percent in normal woman or maybe more  educated women take more vitamins and  also do other things that reduce risk of  birth defects if that is the case then  we would expect to find a lower risk of  birth defects in folic acid users even  if folic acid had no effect on the risk  of birth defects  or it might be the other way around  maybe women with a history of birth  defects take more vitamins and these  women are at a higher risk of having a  baby with birth defects if that were the  case then our analysis good  underestimate the beneficial effect of  folic acid on birth defects folic acid  might reduce the risk by say 50% but  because the high risk women are more  likely to take folic acid we only see a  35% lower risk  like any observational study this one  may be affected by compounding which  means that in our analysis we will need  to adjust for variables like education  and prior history or birth defects and  in particular it turns out that there is  a variable L in our dataset and if we  adjust for L the estimate moves from a  35% reduction to only a 20% reduction in  risk that's a big change so do we need  to adjust for L then what is the closest  to the true effect of folic acid the  unadjusted estimate of 35 percent lower  risk or the L adjusted estimate of 20  percent lower risk questions like this  arise constantly in research studies and  by the end of this lesson you will know  how to handle these questions you will  be able to explain under which  circumstances you would want to adjust  for a very well L to quantify the effect  of folic acid on the risk of neuron to  defense
 

